    "global": {
      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "For a nonempty finite sample with both coefficients free, a finite MLE exists uniquely if every distinct value occurs fewer than $n/2$ times. Two distinct values occurring equally often also attain a maximum, with a continuum of maximizing pairs. In every other sample with an atom of multiplicity at least $n/2$, no finite MLE exists. With location held, a unique finite scale MLE exists exactly when fewer than $n/2$ observations equal that location. With positive scale held, a location maximum always exists but need not be unique. Holding both coefficients leaves likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "At an atom of multiplicity $k$, collapse has likelihood proportional to $\\sigma^{n-2k}$. When all $2k<n$, the likelihood tends to zero at every boundary, so a maximum is attained in the interior. If $2k=n$ at $a$, put $w=\\mu+i\\sigma$ and $v=-1/(w-a)$. Writing $t=\\operatorname{Im}v$, the likelihood is $\\pi^{-n}t^n/\\prod_{x_i\\ne a}|1+(x_i-a)v|^2$. Each denominator factor is at least $(x_i-a)^2t^2$. The resulting upper bound is attained only if all observations other than $a$ have the same value $b$, with $\\operatorname{Re}v=-1/(b-a)$. Otherwise it is approached only at collapse. With location held, the log-scale score $n-2\\sum_i\\sigma^2/(\\sigma^2+(x_i-\\mu)^2)$ decreases strictly from $n-2k$ to $-n$ when some residual is nonzero. With scale held, continuity and decay at location infinity give attainment, without strict concavity in location. For observations $-a,a$ and held $0<\\sigma<a$, the location maxima are $\\pm\\sqrt{a^2-\\sigma^2}$. For joint uniqueness, join any two upper-half-plane points $(\\mu,\\sigma)$ by a vertical line or a circle orthogonal to the real axis. On a vertical line, put $\\sigma=e^t$. Each negative log-density term, apart from a constant, is $\\log(e^t+(x_i-\\mu)^2e^{-t})$, strictly convex unless $x_i=\\mu$. On a circle with centre $c$ and radius $r>0$, put $\\mu=c+r\\tanh t$ and $\\sigma=r/\\cosh t$. Each term becomes $\\log(A_i\\cosh t-B_i\\sinh t)$, where $A_i=((x_i-c)^2+r^2)/r$ and $B_i=2(x_i-c)$. Its second derivative is $(A_i^2-B_i^2)/(A_i\\cosh t-B_i\\sinh t)^2\\ge0$, with equality only at $x_i=c-r$ or $x_i=c+r$. At least three distinct observations therefore make the sum strictly convex along every such connecting path, excluding two joint maxima. The strict sub-half-mass condition requires at least three distinct values."
    },
